MLLM description: The Brown-headed Cowbird is a medium-sized bird with a slender body and a long tail. Its most distinctive feature is its brown head, which contrasts with its dark brown to black body. The bird has a short, pointed beak that is slightly curved at the tip, ideal for probing into grass and seeds. The legs are relatively short and strong, allowing it to walk and forage efficiently.

In terms of color patterns and markings, the Brown-headed Cowbird's plumage is predominantly dark brown or black, with some individuals having a lighter brown or grayish wash on their back and wings. The underparts are usually lighter than the upper parts, often appearing pale brown or buff-colored. The eye is dark and surrounded by a thin white ring, which is a characteristic feature of this species.

The size and proportions of the Brown-headed Cowbird make it appear compact and sturdy. It is generally smaller than a crow but larger than a sparrow, with a wingspan of around 10-12 inches.

