MLLM description: The Black-backed Woodpecker is a medium-sized woodpecker with a robust body, short neck, and a long, pointed bill. Its most distinctive feature is its black back and wings, contrasting sharply with its white underparts. The head is black with a white stripe running through the eye, and a yellow patch on the nape. The tail is black with white bars. The bird's legs are strong and dark, adapted for clinging to tree trunks. It has a short, stiff tail that it uses for support while climbing. The Black-backed Woodpecker is often seen drumming on trees, using its bill to create resonant sounds. It is a solitary or pair-bonded species, and its diet consists mainly of insects, which it finds by pecking at tree bark.
